# Sub-tick interpolation of the animated terrain
# (tests/harness/check_feeder_subtick.sh --game spyro-2 --path terrain).
#   <source> | <V> | [rect=<x0>,<y0>,<x1>,<y1> ...] [step=<px>] [itol=<px>]
#
# This path's knob, OPENPETE_TERRAIN_VANIM, does not suppress the terrain
# feed: it snaps the vertex channel of the sub-tick present to the newest
# tick's state. The canonical presents are therefore identical between the two
# legs and only the intermediate present differs, so the legs are intermediate
# presents alone and their difference is exactly the pixels the vertex
# interpolation moved. Both legs select the terrain path, so no other producer
# can enter it.
#
# Read per cell: the differing-pixel count at each alpha. alpha 1 must differ
# in no pixel (every interpolation term is zero at the tick's own instant),
# alpha 0 must differ in more than `step` pixels (the channel moved something
# this cell can see), and alpha 0.5 separates a channel that carries the
# interval from one that holds its tick state: the latter renders the same
# present in both legs and the count collapses to zero.
#
# The whole frame is the cell where no rect is named: the render-path
# selection has already isolated the terrain.
#
# colossus-moving-terrain: the door Spyro faces at the end of the movie,
# sector 29, swinging open. Its HP vertex bank changes in RAM on every tick
# from VBL 2000 to 2118 (58 of its 86 HP vertices move between the ticks at
# VBL 2060 and 2062; the LP bank does not move and its +0x18 pending byte
# holds the LP descriptor index); the terrain-only render path draws it. The
# sector is named by keyframe descriptors in both poly-anim lists of the
# registry (D_ENV_ANIM_REGISTRY +0x10 LP index 16, +0x20 HP index 18). The
# two rows sit one third and two thirds into the swing.
colossus-moving-terrain.bk2 | 2040 |
colossus-moving-terrain.bk2 | 2080 |
